R["«O72»"] = """**(c)** The pre-specified primary metric, **conversion, rises from 7.31% to 7.94%: +0.63 points, +8.6% relative (95% CI +0.32 to +0.94 points; Holm p = 0.0004)**. Orders per user (+8.9%) and add-to-cart rate (+5.9%) agree. **Revenue per user is not clearly higher**: winsorised +₹4.36 (CI -₹0.81 to +₹9.53, Holm p = 0.39). The raw difference of ₹78.41 (CI ₹8.84 to ₹147.99) is driven by **25 outliers, 18 in treatment and 7 in control**; winsorising cuts the standard error from 35.5 to 2.6. Wishlist adds and bounce rate show no change. **Guardrails:** page load is **+21.7 ms (+1.0%)**, non-inferior to a 3% margin; bounce is non-inferior; support contacts are **+0.14 points (+4.9%)**, non-inferior only just (upper bound 0.0030 equals the margin, p = 0.047). **Heterogeneity:** no moderator is significant (new user p = 0.54, platform p = 0.94, city tier p = 0.96), though the lift is larger for new users (+10.6% vs +7.7%) with overlapping intervals. **(f)** We can claim a conversion gain; we cannot claim a revenue gain. The support-contact guardrail needs watching in the ramp."""
R["«O73»"] = """CUPED with pre-period spend reduces variance by only **3.8% for conversion and 2.6% for revenue** (correlation 0.196 for conversion), and the full ANCOVA by **7.6% and 4.9%**. That is much less than the 30 to 50% often quoted, because pre-spend predicts a 14-day outcome only weakly here. The adjusted effects are close to the raw ones (conversion +0.0065 CUPED and +0.0067 ANCOVA against +0.0063 raw; revenue +₹4.72 and +₹4.90 against +₹4.36). In 400 **A/A tests** the false-positive rate is **4.3% (raw) and 5.3% (CUPED) for conversion, 5.5% and 6.0% for revenue**, all within what 400 splits can tell apart from 5%, and mean estimates are near zero, so the adjustment does not bias the result. Adjustment is safe but gives a modest gain on this data."""
R["«O74»"] = """with 113,562 users over 14 days (about 8,100 a day) and baseline conversion of 7.31%, a 50/50 split can detect a **relative lift of 5.9%** at 80% power, a 20/80 split **7.4%** and a 10/90 split **9.9%**. Power to detect a +5% lift is only **66%** at 50/50 (47% at 20% treatment, 29% at 10%), so the Flipkart-style 10% ramp alone cannot confirm small wins. Planning numbers: **+5% conversion needs 79,567 users per arm (19.6 days)**, +10% needs 19,892 (4.9 days), +2% needs 497,294 (123 days). Revenue is far costlier: +5% needs 165,848 per arm raw (41 days) or 157,733 with ANCOVA (39 days), because revenue SD is 5.1 times its mean. The observed +8.6% conversion lift is above the 5.9% MDE, consistent with its significance; the revenue estimate was underpowered."""
R["«O75»"] = """with five looks, **naive peeking at 1.96 gives a false-positive rate of 14.3%** against 5.1% for a single final test. All three corrections hold the rate near or below 5%: Bonferroni 3.3%, Pocock 4.9% and **O'Brien-Fleming 5.1%**. The price is power: **79.0% for O'Brien-Fleming**, 70.7% for Pocock and 64.5% for Bonferroni, against 80.2% for the fixed test. O'Brien-Fleming also stops early on average (it uses 80% of the planned sample, Pocock 71%). Naive peeking has high power (84.9%) only because it also rejects too often under no effect. O'Brien-Fleming boundaries start very strict (4.56 at the first look) and end near 2.04, so early looks rarely stop the test."""
R["«O76»"] = """a user-level A/B shows **+7.2% completed trips (CI +4.9% to +9.5%)**, against a true global-rollout lift of **+4.1%**; its interval does not contain the truth. It also shows **no change in success rate (+0.05 points, CI -0.37 to +0.38)** while the truth is **-1.49 points**, so it misses the harm to the shared pool. The pooled treated-minus-control comparison across minutes gives +1.9% and ignores time effects. Switchbacks give **+2.9% (no washout) and +3.1% (10-minute washout)**, with intervals (1.4% to 4.4% and 1.4% to 4.8%) that include 4.1%. Both switchbacks **understate** the lift and show the success-rate loss only partly (-0.73 and -0.80 points). This is a single run, so section 7.7 repeats it."""
R["«O77»"] = """across 120 simulated worlds, **alternating 60-minute slots are the worst design**: bias **-3.3 points**, coverage of the truth **0%**, and an A/A false-positive rate of **100%** (mean A/A estimate -1.6%), because the slots line up with the 120-minute supply cycle. Alternating 53-minute slots still have bias -1.9 and 0% coverage; random 53-minute slots have bias -0.8 and 81% coverage. **Paired-random 53-minute slots with a 10-minute washout** have bias -0.6, RMSE 0.87, **92% coverage** and an A/A false-positive rate of 4.2%; a 20-minute washout does slightly better (bias -0.27, 93% coverage). Interval length matters: **29 minutes has only 13% coverage** and 112 minutes has 96% coverage but wide intervals (5.1 points) and 84% power. The user-level A/B is biased by **+2.0 points** with 57% coverage. Longer tests narrow the intervals (CI width 6.8 to 2.8 points from 7 to 28 days; power 51% to 100%) but **do not remove the bias (about -0.6 points)**. Washout reduces bias but does not eliminate it here, so switchback estimates should be read as a lower bound."""
R["«O8»"] = """a gradient-boosted prediction of the outcome from pre-period features explains little (out-of-fold **R² = 0.081 for conversion, 0.049 for revenue**). **CUPAC reduces variance by 8.1% for conversion and 4.9% for revenue**, against 7.6% and 4.9% for the linear ANCOVA, so the machine-learning model adds **almost nothing** over regression on this data. Effects barely move (conversion +0.0063 raw, +0.0067 ANCOVA and CUPAC; revenue +₹4.36, +₹4.90 and +₹5.10). The honest conclusion is that the pre-period covariates are the limit; a stronger model cannot create information the features do not hold. Richer behavioural history would be the first thing to test."""
